Files Used vs. Not Used in Your AI Chess System
Your AI chess system follows this pipeline:

Capture an image of the chessboard.
Detect chess piece presence and determine the opponent's move (human).
Convert the detected move to UCI format.
Pass the move to Stockfish, which calculates the AI move.
Apply the move and repeat.
🔹 Files That Are USED in Your System
These files directly contribute to the vision-based AI chess system.

1️⃣ Camera & Image Processing
✅ chess_camera.py → Captures the chessboard image using a camera.
✅ chessboard_perspective_transform.py → Corrects camera perspective to align the board properly.

2️⃣ Board & Piece Detection
✅ chessboard_frame.py → Extracts individual squares from the chessboard image.
✅ square.py → Represents an individual square on the chessboard.
✅ square_color_detector.py → Detects pieces on the board using machine learning.
✅ helpers.py → Identifies legal moves based on the detected board state.

3️⃣ Move Handling & AI Decision Making
✅ agent.py → Manages the decision-making process. It identifies the human move and passes it to Stockfish.
✅ stockfish_player.py → Interfaces with Stockfish, gets AI's move based on the opponent’s move.

4️⃣ Game State Management
✅ __init__.py (game management) → Reads and writes game state (PGN format).
✅ raspberryturkd.py → Runs the AI system in a continuous loop.

🔻 Files That Are NOT Used
These files are not needed for your vision-based chess system but may have been useful for a different project.

❌ collection.py → Used for collecting and saving random chessboard images. (Only needed for dataset creation, not real-time play.)
❌ constants.py → Defines SQUARE_SIZE and BOARD_SIZE. (Could be replaced with hardcoded values, but keeping it is fine.)
❌ raw_pixels_extractor.py → Extracts raw pixel features, but we are using square_color_detector instead.
❌ human_player.py → Allows a human to input moves via UNIX socket, not needed since the human moves physically.
❌ player.py → Just a base class for chess players, not necessary.
❌ dataset.py → Handles dataset saving/loading, only useful for training models.
❌ create_dataset.py → Used for creating datasets, not needed for live gameplay.

🔹 What You Need to ADD to Make This Work
Your system is almost complete, but you still need a few things:

1️⃣ Better Move Detection Algorithm
Right now, possible_moves_for_board() assumes that only one move is possible.
What if multiple pieces could have moved?
Solution: Implement a piece tracking system that compares the current board with the previous one and finds exactly which piece moved.
2️⃣ Improve Camera Calibration
If your camera is slightly misaligned, piece detection will fail.
Solution: Modify recalibrate_camera_position() in chessboard_perspective_transform.py to make calibration more robust.
3️⃣ Handle Game Start & End
What happens when the game starts? Is Stockfish playing White or Black?
What happens when the game ends? Should it restart?
Solution: Modify raspberryturkd.py to handle game start/reset logic.
4️⃣ Debugging & Testing
Print out intermediate steps (like detected board states, moves, etc.).
Use test images of chessboards and compare detected vs. actual moves.
🔹 Final Steps
Integrate everything into one script to run the full pipeline.
Test piece recognition with a real chessboard.
Fine-tune move detection to handle tricky cases (castling, en passant).
Improve robustness (handle camera shifts, lighting changes, etc.).
